cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  bert-lenses: a systems-modeling instrument that refuses
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it. The machine-checked claims it makes
  are pinned to a specific commit of systems-science-foundations; cite that
  commit alongside this software when the proofs are what you are relying on.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Shingai
    family-names: Thornton
    affiliation: Halcyonic Systems
repository-code: https://github.com/halcyonic-systems/bert-lenses
abstract: >-
  A modeling instrument in which a formal kernel judges whether an authored
  model holds as a system under three traditions of systems science (Klir,
  Bunge, Mobus), citing the precondition behind every verdict. Models are
  authored on a canvas or in SL, a deterministic line-oriented system language;
  the two round-trip through one neutral model. Where a model holds it can be
  run under a model-declared invariant. The kernel is Rust compiled to
  WebAssembly and owns all formalism; the lens projections are grounded in
  machine-checked Lean 4 proofs, pinned by commit and gated in CI.
keywords:
  - systems science
  - systems modeling
  - formal methods
  - Lean 4
  - Klir
  - Bunge
  - Mobus
  - WebAssembly
license: MIT

references:
  - type: software
    title: >-
      systems-science-foundations: a Lean 4 formalization of the Klir-Bunge-Mobus
      common core
    authors:
      - given-names: Shingai
        family-names: Thornton
        affiliation: Halcyonic Systems
    repository-code: https://github.com/halcyonic-systems/systems-science-foundations
    notes: >-
      The proof base. bert-lenses' machine-checked claims are pinned to commit
      71a7883b3bbe15b91ad137b81bb80a77915e0da1; each claim resolves to a named
      Lean declaration through docs/lean-manifest.json, and CI fails if a cited
      symbol stops resolving with its declared kind. Cite this when the proofs
      are what you are relying on, not the tool.
